STORY RULES (very important for 20+ rapid-fire news in 50-60 seconds):
- NEVER say a topic label like "खेल में", "स्वास्थ्य:", "राजनीति में", "रक्षा मोर्चे पर". Just say the news.
- Each story = 5–8 Hindi words in ONE crisp, punchy sentence:
  State WHO/WHAT happened and the key outcome directly in rapid-fire TV headline style.
  Good: "RBI ने रेपो रेट 5.5% पर बरकरार रखा।"
  Good: "पीएम मोदी ने 12 हज़ार करोड़ की सड़क परियोजनाओं का उद्घाटन किया।"
  Good: "बांकेबिहारी मंदिर कॉरिडोर का नया प्रस्ताव मंजूर हुआ।"
  Good: "इसरो ने नया संचार उपग्रह सफलतापूर्वक लॉन्च किया।"
- Each story has ONE clear part or at most two ultra-fast parts:
  Each part = 4–7 spoken words, and its picture must show EXACTLY what THAT part says.
  THE PICTURE TEST (this is the channel's #1 quality rule — a photo of the wrong thing is worse than no photo):
  read your "say" line, then name the ONE object/person/place in it. "visual" and "entity" must describe that exact same thing.
  If the line says "बांकेबिहारी मंदिर" the entity is "Banke Bihari Temple" — never a generic modern building or stock office.
  * Each part has:
      "say":    the spoken words (Devanagari; ISRO, RBI, IPL, GDP in English letters OK; numbers as spoken)
      "visual": one English sentence naming the ONE main subject of THIS "say" line and its action/setting
                (e.g. "Banke Bihari temple in Vrindavan with traditional Indian architecture"), Indian setting for Indian news
      "entity": exact English Wikipedia title ONLY for a real specific person/organisation/place/vehicle
                ("Banke Bihari Temple", "Narendra Modi", "Reserve Bank of India", "Donald Trump", "Vande Bharat Express"), else ""
      "search": 2–4 English stock-photo words taken FROM the "say" line's main noun
                ("banke bihari temple vrindavan", "gold coins jewellery", "indian railway station")
      "source": "entity" | "stock" | "ai"
  Never reuse the same "search" for two different stories; never leave "visual" or "search" empty.
- "on_screen_text": 2–4 Hindi words headline chip for the story.
- "src": the candidate number [n].

